2. Draw a circle of radius 2 inches to represent the base of the truncated cone (as shown in passage 1).
3. Draw a second circle of radius 0.25 inches on top of the first circle to represent the circular top of the truncated cone (as shown in passage 2).
4. Extend the cone up to a point using the "Line" tool, making sure to maintain a constant angle between the sides (as shown in passage 3).
5. Label the diameter of the base as "b" and the diameter of the top as "t". Also, label the height of the truncated cone as "h".

Here is the GeoGebra code to create the truncated cone:
```scss
geometry = Cone();
geometry.setBase(Circle(2, "b"));
geometry.setTop(Circle(0.25, "t"));
geometry.setHeight(7, "h");
```
Note that the "Cone" function in GeoGebra creates a truncated cone by default, so you do not need to specify the "truncated" option.
